# Independently authored CREATE EXTENSION WITH-clause
# CORRUPTION-class silent-drop + uuid-ossp self-
# contradictory dash-reject sentinel slice for the GPL
# MTR-format runner. NOT copied or adapted from MySQL's
# mysql-test/ or PG regress.
#
# Adjacent companion slices (do NOT duplicate):
#   - create_extension_basic_extra.test:79-119 +
#     .result:11-17 pins the BARE-form whitelist:
#     `CREATE EXTENSION citext` (and the five other
#     whitelisted names, including the QUOTED
#     `"uuid-ossp"` form) silently succeeds. Unknown
#     names error 1105 with the hand-rolled em-dash
#     formatter. IF NOT EXISTS swallows the unknown
#     1105. DROP EXTENSION is ALWAYS a no-op. NONE of
#     those shapes carry a WITH clause; the bare-form
#     slice never probes SCHEMA / VERSION / CASCADE
#     against a WHITELISTED extension.
#   - pg_fdw_extension_grammar_sentinel_basic_extra
#     (PR #465) pins the WITH-keyword grammar
#     bifurcation against an UNKNOWN extension
#     (`CREATE EXTENSION foo SCHEMA s1` → 1064 at
#     parser; `CREATE EXTENSION foo WITH SCHEMA s1
#     VERSION '1.0' CASCADE` → 1105 at dispatcher
#     because `foo` is not whitelisted). This slice
#     does NOT re-pin either; the novel evidence is
#     what happens when the WITH-prefix branch
#     consumes the option list AND the extension
#     name IS whitelisted — the dispatcher returns
#     OK and the options are SILENTLY DROPPED.
#     PR #465 proved the WITH-form parses; this PR
#     proves the WITH-form CORRUPTS.
#   - uuid_basic_extra / uuid_generate_v1_basic_extra
#     pin uuid-ossp FUNCTIONS (UUID_GENERATE_V*) but
#     not the `CREATE EXTENSION uuid-ossp` statement
#     itself (the bare slice uses the QUOTED
#     `"uuid-ossp"` form, never the unquoted form).
#
#   Pre-author dedup grep on tests/main/*.test for
#   `CREATE EXTENSION.*(WITH|SCHEMA|VERSION|CASCADE)|
#   CREATE EXTENSION citext|CREATE EXTENSION pgcrypto|
#   CREATE EXTENSION uuid|IF NOT EXISTS.*citext`:
#   THREE hits — create_extension_basic_extra (bare
#   whitelist), pg_fdw_extension_grammar_sentinel
#   (WITH-clause against UNKNOWN name), uuid_basic /
#   zero_arg_constants (uuid-ossp FUNCTIONS). NONE
#   probe WITH-clauses against a WHITELISTED
#   extension; NONE probe the unquoted dash-form
#   `CREATE EXTENSION uuid-ossp`. Per
#   [[feedback_gap_scan_must_grep_all_files]] the wide
#   case-insensitive grep was the full population.
#
# This slice pins THREE structural findings:
#
#   FINDING 1 — CORRUPTION-class silent-drop of
#   WITH-clauses against whitelisted extensions. Zeta
#   has no `CREATE EXTENSION` option semantics at all
#   (the dispatcher does not honor SCHEMA, VERSION, or
#   CASCADE — confirmed by impl comment "Accepted
#   silently so migration scripts (Rails, Django,
#   Alembic) work"). PR #465 proved the WITH-form
#   parses against an UNKNOWN extension (1105 at
#   dispatcher); this slice proves the WITH-form
#   SILENTLY DROPS the entire option list when the
#   extension IS whitelisted. The DAMNING evidence
#   that no validation occurs:
#     * `WITH VERSION '99.99'` (a nonsense version
#       for citext, which has no 99.99 release) →
#       silent OK.
#     * `WITH SCHEMA NOSUCHSCHEMA` (a schema that
#       does not exist in the catalog) → silent OK.
#       Migration scripts that emit
#       `CREATE EXTENSION pgcrypto WITH SCHEMA
#       NOSUCHSCHEMA` get NO indication that the
#       schema-relocation request was ignored AND
#       that the schema does not exist. zeta
#       neither honors the schema target nor
#       validates that it is real.
#   This is the textbook CORRUPTION-class pattern: the
#   client believes the server accepted a constraint
#   (schema, version, dependency-cascade) that the
#   server has no machinery to enforce. zeta's bare-
#   form whitelist gives a useful silent-ack semantic
#   for migration scripts (`extension already supplied,
#   nothing to do`); the WITH-form silent-drop
#   extends that contract into territory the client
#   should NOT be silently acked over.
#
#   FINDING 2 — Self-contradictory help-text vs
#   parser for `uuid-ossp`. Zeta's OWN unknown-
#   extension help-text (pinned at
#   create_extension_basic_extra.result:13) names
#   `uuid-ossp` (with the dash) among the natively-
#   supported set. But the canonical PG spelling
#   `CREATE EXTENSION uuid-ossp` (unquoted, with
#   embedded dash) errors 1064 at the parser layer
#   (`Expected: end of statement, found: - at
#   Line: 1, Column: 22`) — the parser treats `-`
#   as a binary operator after the `uuid` token,
#   never reaching the dispatcher. The dash-
#   containing name is parseable ONLY in the quoted
#   form `"uuid-ossp"` (as
#   create_extension_basic_extra:82 uses). zeta's
#   help-text is therefore self-contradictory: it
#   advertises a name that its own parser refuses
#   to accept in the spelling apps actually use.
#   PG accepts BOTH `CREATE EXTENSION uuid-ossp`
#   AND `CREATE EXTENSION "uuid-ossp"` (the dash
#   is a legal identifier character in PG's
#   extension-name lexer, distinct from the
#   subtraction operator). Migration scripts and
#   pg_dump output use the UNQUOTED dash form; the
#   QUOTED form is a zeta-only workaround that is
#   undocumented in zeta's own help-text.
#
#   FINDING 3 — Companion to PR #465's bare-form
#   grammar bifurcation. PR #465 (FDW slice) +
#   create_extension_basic_extra together establish
#   that zeta has THREE distinct CREATE EXTENSION
#   surfaces:
#     1. bare form, whitelisted name → silent OK
#     2. bare form, unknown name → 1105 dispatcher
#     3. bare form + trailing option WITHOUT WITH
#        → 1064 parser (any token, any extension
#        name)
#   This slice adds the FOURTH and FIFTH surfaces:
#     4. WITH form, whitelisted name, ANY options
#        (including nonsense) → silent OK; options
#        SILENTLY DROPPED.
#     5. unquoted dash-name form (`uuid-ossp`) →
#        1064 parser, even though the same name
#        appears in zeta's own help-text and works
#        in the quoted form.
#   Combined with PR #465's surface 6 (WITH form,
#   unknown name → 1105 dispatcher), zeta exposes
#   FIVE distinct failure-vs-dispatch surfaces for
#   a single PG statement. Apps porting from PG
#   that emit any of {bare-unknown, bare-with-
#   trailing-opt, WITH-with-options-they-care-
#   about, unquoted-dash-name} get DIFFERENT
#   error fingerprints — or worse, SILENT
#   CORRUPTION — for SYNTACTICALLY-EQUIVALENT or
#   SEMANTICALLY-IMPORTANT spellings.
#
# Big-picture finding:
#
#   zeta has NO CREATE EXTENSION option semantics. The
#   bare-form whitelist is a useful silent-ack for
#   migration scripts; the WITH-form silent-drop is
#   a CORRUPTION-class extension of that contract
#   into option territory where the silent-ack
#   semantics are no longer safe. Combined with the
#   uuid-ossp parser self-contradiction, this is the
#   parser-and-dispatcher-layer analog of
#   [[project_two_layer_type_recognition_pattern]]:
#   the same name is fingerprinted differently
#   depending on quoting and option-list spelling,
#   with the corrupting branch being the one apps
#   most commonly emit. See also
#   [[project_option_registry_split]] and
#   [[project_function_arms_gap_closed]] — the
#   option-arm coverage gap is wide open for DDL
#   options.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN BUG (CORRUPTION-class) — for
#     whitelisted extensions, `CREATE EXTENSION
#     <name> WITH SCHEMA s VERSION 'v' CASCADE`
#     silently drops the ENTIRE option list. The
#     SCHEMA target is NOT honored AND is NOT
#     validated (NOSUCHSCHEMA is accepted). The
#     VERSION pin is NOT honored AND is NOT
#     validated (`'99.99'` for citext is accepted).
#     CASCADE has no dependencies to act on (zeta
#     never loads extensions). Do not rely on any
#     of those clauses; assume the install was
#     bare-form.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN GAP — `CREATE EXTENSION
#     uuid-ossp` (unquoted, with embedded dash)
#     errors 1064 at parser. Use the quoted form
#     `CREATE EXTENSION "uuid-ossp"` — the
#     quoted form is the only spelling zeta's
#     parser accepts for dash-containing
#     extension names. zeta's own help-text
#     names `uuid-ossp` without warning that
#     quoting is required.

--echo === SHAPE A — WITH-clause silent-drop against WHITELISTED extension (CORRUPTION) ===

# Baseline — bare-form whitelisted silent OK is
# already pinned at create_extension_basic_extra:85.
# Replayed here ONLY to anchor the contrast with the
# WITH-form variants below.
CREATE EXTENSION citext;

# Each WITH-clause variant SILENTLY succeeds — the
# SCHEMA / VERSION / CASCADE options are dropped on
# the way through the dispatcher. The dispatcher
# returns OK because the extension name IS in the
# whitelist; the option list never reaches any
# validator (there is none).
CREATE EXTENSION citext WITH SCHEMA pg_catalog;
CREATE EXTENSION citext WITH VERSION '1.0';
CREATE EXTENSION citext WITH CASCADE;

# DAMNING — nonsense VERSION accepted. citext has no
# 99.99 release; PG would reject with
# `ERROR: extension "citext" has no version "99.99"`.
# zeta drops the clause and returns OK.
CREATE EXTENSION citext WITH VERSION '99.99';

# Three-option chain — SAME silent drop.
CREATE EXTENSION citext WITH SCHEMA pg_catalog VERSION '1.0' CASCADE;

--echo === SHAPE B — WITH SCHEMA against a NONEXISTENT schema (HEADLINE CORRUPTION) ===

# The MOST damning evidence that zeta does no option
# validation at all. The schema NOSUCHSCHEMA is not
# in the catalog; PG would reject with
# `ERROR: schema "nosuchschema" does not exist`.
# zeta accepts the statement silently — the
# migration script believes pgcrypto was relocated
# to a schema that does not exist. Combined with
# Shape A's nonsense-VERSION acceptance, this
# establishes that zeta has ZERO option-validation
# machinery for CREATE EXTENSION.
CREATE EXTENSION pgcrypto WITH SCHEMA NOSUCHSCHEMA;

--echo === SHAPE C — IF NOT EXISTS variants (same WITH-clause silent-drop) ===

# IF NOT EXISTS is orthogonal to the WITH-clause
# silent-drop. The bare-form IF NOT EXISTS swallow
# is pinned at create_extension_basic_extra:90-91;
# the novel evidence here is that adding a WITH
# clause to an IF NOT EXISTS variant does NOT
# change the behavior — still silent, still drops
# the options.
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS citext;
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS citext WITH SCHEMA pg_catalog;

--echo === SHAPE D — Self-contradictory uuid-ossp parser-reject (unquoted dash form) ===

# zeta's OWN unknown-extension help-text (pinned at
# create_extension_basic_extra.result:13) names
# `uuid-ossp` with the dash among the natively-
# supported set. But the canonical PG spelling
# `CREATE EXTENSION uuid-ossp` (unquoted) errors
# 1064 at the parser — the dash is treated as a
# binary operator after the `uuid` token. The
# parser never reaches the dispatcher; the
# help-text's claim that `uuid-ossp` is supported
# is contradicted by the parser's refusal to
# accept the canonical spelling.
--error 1064
CREATE EXTENSION uuid-ossp;

# The quoted-identifier workaround DOES parse and
# DOES reach the dispatcher's whitelist — pinned
# at create_extension_basic_extra:82. Replayed here
# ONLY to anchor the contrast: the SAME name, in
# QUOTED form, silently succeeds. zeta's quoting
# is the only escape hatch for dash-containing
# extension names.
CREATE EXTENSION "uuid-ossp";

--echo === SHAPE E — DROP EXTENSION baseline (already pinned, anchor only) ===

# DROP EXTENSION is ALWAYS a no-op — pinned at
# create_extension_basic_extra:125-129. Replayed
# once here ONLY to leave the test environment
# clean and to anchor that the WITH-clause
# silent-drop is on the CREATE side; DROP has no
# WITH-clause grammar at all in PG, so there is no
# analogous DROP shape to pin.
DROP EXTENSION citext;
